Distinguish formal charge, oxidation state, and delocalized bonding (@gc-resonance: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/IUPAC-Red-Book.lingua#r_0CW26ZVGP8AR7QH2KXCM36MB4J", #source "../sources/MIT-5-111SC.lingua#r_312Z44VT67CCX60KG27PWM6NZ8", #general-chemistry "Geometry-polarity-and-properties.lingua#r_1ESH4EFVR6399WYBN1405EJ38F") { r_43EAP1EC5N8J3HRPWV4D9T51AF
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 7’s formal-charge, resonance, and octet-exception sections; [Nomenclature of Inorganic Chemistry — IUPAC Red Book and brief guide](../sources/IUPAC-Red-Book.lingua#r_0CW26ZVGP8AR7QH2KXCM36MB4J), the relevant charge/oxidation-state conventions; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.111SC, Fall 2014](../sources/MIT-5-111SC.lingua#r_312Z44VT67CCX60KG27PWM6NZ8), the corresponding Unit II lectures/problems.

Formal charge partitions bonding electrons equally in a Lewis bookkeeping scheme. Oxidation state follows a different assignment convention. Partial charge describes a model or inference about electronic distribution and depends on its definition. A species' total charge constrains these accounts but does not make each atomic assignment interchangeable.

Resonance uses several contributing Lewis structures to describe electronic features that one localized drawing represents poorly. The physical state is described by the combined electronic model rather than a molecule repeatedly switching among the contributing diagrams. Relative contribution and measured properties require evidence and a suitable theory.

Compare bond lengths, symmetry, spectra, and reactivity with the proposed description. A drawing with convenient integer charges can hide equivalent bonds or delocalized electrons. Conversely, a resonance notation should not erase a real distinction between constitutional isomers or rapidly interconverting chemical species; those are different structural questions.

**Work:** analyze a polyatomic ion and a conjugated molecule from the book, computing electron counts, formal charges, and oxidation states separately. Explain a measured or textbook-reported equivalence of bonds without calling the drawings separate chemical substances. State what an experiment would need to distinguish alternative connectivities.

Return to the full naming and bonding sources when a charge label is ambiguous. Later molecular orbitals, acid/base behavior, coordination, and organic chemistry reuse these distinctions. Preserve their definitions so overlapping fields can share the same Record rather than inherit incompatible shorthand.

